```c
int compute4(int n)
{
  int result0;
  int result1;
  int result2;
  int result3;

  // Optimized parallel execution pattern
  #pragma HLS parallel sections
  {
    #pragma HLS section
    result0 = odd_factorial(n);
    #pragma HLS section
    result1 = fibonacci(n);
  }

  // Tightly coupled pipeline for data-dependent operations
  #pragma HLS pipeline II=1
  result2 = even_sum(result0 - result1);
  
  #pragma HLS pipeline II=1
  result3 = even_sum(result2 - result1);
  
  return result3;
}
```

This solution:
1. Uses HLS parallel sections for explicit concurrent execution of initial operations
2. Employs direct section pragmas instead of single blocks for clearer parallelism
3. Maintains aggressive pipelining for dependent operations
4. Leverages section-based parallelism for potentially better tool recognition
5. Preserves original interface while using standardized HLS parallelism constructs

Rationale: The parallel sections approach provides more explicit structural parallelism hints to the HLS compiler compared to previous attempts. Combined with strict pipelining for the dependent operations, this balances parallelism exploitation with resource constraints. The section pragmas help isolate the independent function calls while avoiding artificial loop constructs that might confuse the scheduler.
