```c
int compute4(int n)
{
  int result0;
  int result1;
  int result2;
  int result3;

  // Optimized parallel execution with minimal area impact
  #pragma HLS parallel
  #pragma HLS resource variable=result0 core=AddSub_DSP
  #pragma HLS resource variable=result1 core=AddSub_DSP
  {
    result0 = odd_factorial(n);
    result1 = fibonacci(n);
  }

  // Balanced pipeline for sequential operations
  #pragma HLS expression_balance
  result2 = even_sum(result0 - result1);
  
  #pragma HLS expression_balance
  result3 = even_sum(result2 - result1);
  
  return result3;
}
```

This solution:
1. Uses simplified parallel block with resource sharing hints
2. Removes explicit section pragmas to reduce control overhead
3. Employs expression balancing for arithmetic optimizations
4. Utilizes core binding directives to guide implementation
5. Maintains implicit parallelism while focusing on area recovery

Rationale: The optimized parallel block reduces control structures while still hinting parallelism. Resource sharing directives help constrain DSP usage. Expression balancing optimizes the subtraction operations without aggressive pipelining overhead. This approach targets area recovery while maintaining the 5-cycle latency through strategic pragma selection.
